n mars  is  can we muster the  will  and the funding  and the engineering expertise to  actually bring these samples home  right now there are plans on the drawing  board there are speeches there are  intentions  but  it's a long time between now and the  2030s when these missions that are being  projected  uh being talked about to bring these  things home  in april the ingenuity helicopter became  the first craft to fly in the atmosphere  of another planet this technology  demonstration was one for the history  books  this helicopter which was initially uh  officially designed to carry out just  five flights as a technology  demonstration has been uh flying all it  seems sometimes continuously  the next flights tested the helicopter's  ability to move horizontally and it  covered longer distances in each  subsequent flight ingenuity has now  flown 18 times when we get over to the  delta ingenuity can again be used as a  scout to really point perseverance to  the best places scientifically  to go i think if she's still living we  have lots of opportunities for scouting  as we as we move forward in the mission  nasa selected jezero crater as  perseverance's landing site because it  gives the rover access to a large stack  of rocks that sits at the end of a dry  river valley  based on satellite images scientists  have theorized that these rocks are made  of sediment deposited by an ancient  river that flowed into a lake roughly  3.5 billion years ago so jezreel crater  is essentially if you're if you're  looking for signs of ancient microbial  life on mars this is a place to go it's  the location location location for  science for finding it because  of that delta that we see there nasa  selected its landing site because  scientists have long suspected the area  was once covered in water and therefore  had the potential for the development of  life as we know it but they only could  tell this in target this in the broadest  outlines from orbit  now they have boots on 